<Project>

  <!--
    PublicApi.props — the machine inventory gate of the public surface, shared by every packable
    assembly that has one. A project imports this file with a single line, next to the import of
    build/Packaging.props.

    What it buys: the public surface of the assembly is DECLARED, not implied. Every public type
    and member is listed in PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt next to the project sources, and the analyzer
    enforces both directions as build errors (RS0016 — a public member missing from the inventory,
    RS0017 — an inventory entry with no member behind it; both raised to error by the
    WarningsAsErrors property below). So the surface cannot grow or shrink silently: it either shows
    up as a line in the diff of that file, or the build breaks.

    PublicAPI.Shipped.txt is empty everywhere: nothing has been published yet. Moving lines from
    Unshipped to Shipped is an act of a release, not of ordinary work.

    Who does NOT import it — a closed list, and the reason is not "no public surface":
      * the six migrations assemblies, *.Migrations.PostgreSql and *.Migrations.SqlServer for each of
        the transaction, OpenIddict and audit stores: EF Core generates their migrations as public
        classes, so a surface exists — but it is produced by a tool and grows with every
        `dotnet ef migrations add`. The inventory would have to be extended on each of them while
        protecting nothing: those files are not written by hand in the first place;
      * the metapackage Veriqa.Core.BaseChannels: it ships dependencies only
        (IncludeBuildOutput=false), so there is no assembly to inventory.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      RS0026/RS0027 are a different subject from the inventory gate and are switched off here for
      the whole set, in one place rather than per project. They are advice about EVOLVING an
      already published API: do not add a second overload with optional parameters, and keep the
      overload with the most parameters as the one carrying them. They fire on overload shapes that
      already exist and are deliberate (an operation offered with a context and with a scope, each
      taking an optional CancellationToken — the ordinary .NET shape), and the compatibility they
      protect does not exist yet: PublicAPI.Shipped.txt is empty everywhere, nothing has been
      released, so there is no consumer whose call could become ambiguous. Obeying them would mean
      redesigning public overloads — a breaking change for no one's benefit, and not what a hygiene
      task does. RS0016/RS0017, the two rules the gate is about, stay errors.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);RS0026;RS0027</NoWarn>
    <!--
      The two rules the gate is about are raised to error HERE, as an MSBuild property, and not in
      the nested .editorconfig of each project. The nested files also carry a severity line for
      them, and it is enough for RS0016 but not for RS0017: an .editorconfig severity is resolved
      per syntax tree, and RS0017 is reported on a position inside PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt, which is
      an AdditionalFile and not a syntax tree — so no section, [*.cs] or [PublicAPI.*.txt], reaches
      it. That is how half the gate stayed a warning while the file above claimed it was an error:
      RS0016 broke the build, RS0017 printed 23 stale entries and let it pass. WarningsAsErrors is
      applied by the compiler to the diagnostic regardless of where it is reported, so it covers
      both directions.
    -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);RS0016;RS0017</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="5.6.0">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
